    "englishTranslation": "Defining Indescribability (Anirvacanīyatva, Part 1): Having established Avidyā and superimposition, Madhusūdana now grounds Advaita's hallmark ontological category—Anirvacanīyatva (indeterminability / being neither ultimately real nor utterly non-existent). Refuting Vyāsatīrtha's complaint that 'being neither sat nor asat' fails for lack of clear definitions, he states the exact definition: 'Absence of both Being (sattva) and Non-Being (asattva)'—or 'Possessing the mutual absolute non-existence of both', where Being (sat) means 'unsublatability in all three times' (trikālābādhyatva) and Non-Being (asat) means 'utter unfitness to be apprehended anywhere as real' (like a hare's horn).\n\nSystematically evaluating five alternative realist proposals for 'Being' (universal sattā-jāti, causal efficacy, unsublatability, epistemic provability, and non-emptiness), he shows how neither sattā-rāhitva nor asattā-rāhitva turns into an ultimate metaphysical reality—just as 'being neither eternal nor non-eternal' holds cleanly of non-temporal universals/prior-absences without self-contradiction!",
